(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_zoology_breathingandexchangeofgases=[{id:"neet-2026-q146",pn:146,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Respiratory volumes",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. ERV (Expiratory Reserve Volume) \u2014 I. 2500 \u2013 3000 mL
B. RV (Residual Volume) \u2014 II. 500 mL
C. IRV (Inspiratory Reserve Volume) \u2014 III. 1000 \u2013 1100 mL
D. TV (Tidal Volume) \u2014 IV. 1100 \u2013 1200 mL`,o:["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV"],c:2,s:`ERV (Expiratory Reserve Volume) is 1000\u20131100 mL.
RV (Residual Volume) is 1100\u20131200 mL.
IRV (Inspiratory Reserve Volume) is 2500\u20133000 mL.
TV (Tidal Volume) is 500 mL.
So A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q154",pn:154,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Respiratory organs in animals",q:"Sponges exchange O\u2082 with CO\u2082 by __________.",o:["moist cuticle","tracheal tubes","gills","simple diffusion over their entire body surfaces"],c:3,s:`Breathing mechanisms differ among animals, mainly by habitat and level of organisation.
Lower invertebrates like sponges, coelenterates and flatworms exchange O\u2082 with CO\u2082 by simple diffusion over their entire body surfaces.
Earthworms use moist cuticle.
Insects have tracheal tubes.
Gills are used by most aquatic arthropods and molluscs.`},{id:"neet-2026-q160",pn:160,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Steps of respiration",q:`In humans, respiration occurs in the following steps. Arrange these steps in the correct order.
A. Diffusion of O\u2082 and CO\u2082 between blood and tissues
B. Diffusion of O\u2082 and CO\u2082 across alveolar membrane
C. Pulmonary ventilation by which atmospheric air is drawn in and CO\u2082 rich alveolar air is released out
D. Cellular respiration
E. Transport of gases by the blood`,o:["A, B, C, D, E","E, A, C, D, B","C, A, B, E, D","C, B, E, A, D"],c:3,s:`Respiration involves these steps in order:
(i) Breathing or pulmonary ventilation: atmospheric air is drawn in and CO\u2082 rich alveolar air is released out (C).
(ii) Diffusion of gases across the alveolar membrane (B).
(iii) Transport of gases by the blood (E).
(iv) Diffusion of O\u2082 and CO\u2082 between blood and tissues (A).
(v) Use of O\u2082 by the cells for catabolic reactions and release of CO\u2082 (D).
Order: C, B, E, A, D.`},{id:"neet-2026-q177",pn:177,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Modes of respiration",q:`Match List I with List II:
A. Molluscs \u2014 I. Pulmonary respiration only
B. Reptiles \u2014 II. Branchial respiration
C. Adult amphibians \u2014 III. Cellular respiration
D. Amoeba \u2014 IV. Pulmonary and cutaneous respiration
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III"],c:1,s:`A. Molluscs perform branchial respiration using feather-like gills (II).
B. Reptiles perform pulmonary respiration only, through lungs (I).
C. Adult amphibians perform pulmonary and cutaneous respiration, through lungs and moist skin (IV).
D. Amoeba performs cellular respiration to generate ATP for survival (III).
Thus A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III.`},{id:"neet-2024-q163",pn:163,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Lung volumes and capacities",q:`Match List I with List II :
A. Expiratory capacity \u2014 I. Expiratory reserve volume + Tidal volume + Inspiratory reserve volume
B. Functional residual capacity \u2014 II. Tidal volume + Expiratory reserve volume
C. Vital capacity \u2014 III. Tidal volume + Inspiratory reserve volume
D. Inspiratory capacity \u2014 IV. Expiratory reserve volume + Residual volume`,o:["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I"],c:2,s:`Expiratory capacity = tidal volume + expiratory reserve volume.
Functional residual capacity = expiratory reserve volume + residual volume.
Vital capacity = expiratory reserve volume + tidal volume + inspiratory reserve volume.
Inspiratory capacity = tidal volume + inspiratory reserve volume.
So A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III.`},{id:"neet-2024-q167",pn:167,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Oxygen transport",q:"Which of the following factors are favourable for the formation of oxyhaemoglobin in alveoli ?",o:["Low pCO\u2082 and High H\u207A concentration","Low pCO\u2082 and High temperature","High pO\u2082 and High pCO\u2082","High pO\u2082 and Lesser H\u207A concentration"],c:3,s:`Option (4) is correct.
Oxyhaemoglobin forms in alveoli when pO\u2082 is high, H\u207A is low, pCO\u2082 is low and temperature is low.
Options (1), (2) and (3) do not favour its formation.`},{id:"neet-2023-q177",pn:177,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Respiratory volumes and capacities",q:"Vital capacity of lung is _________.",o:["IRV + ERV + TV","IRV + ERV","IRV + ERV + TV + RV","IRV + ERV + TV \u2013 RV"],c:0,s:`Vital capacity is the maximum air a person can breathe out after a deep breath in (or breathe in after a forced expiration).
It includes ERV, TV and IRV.
RV is not part of it.`},{id:"neet-2022-q155",pn:155,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Transport of oxygen",q:"Under normal physiological conditions in human being every 100 ml of oxygenated blood can deliver ________ ml of O2 to the tissues.",o:["5 ml","4 ml","10 ml","2 ml"],c:0,s:`Under normal conditions 100 mL of oxygenated blood delivers about 5 mL of O\u2082 to the tissues.
The other values are wrong. (About 4 mL is the CO\u2082 that 100 mL of deoxygenated blood delivers to the alveoli.)`},{id:"neet-2022r-q159",pn:159,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Regulation of respiration",q:"Identify the region of human brain which has pneumotaxic centre that alters respiratory rate by reducing the duration of inspiration.",o:["Cerebrum","Medulla","Pons","Thalamus"],c:2,s:`The pneumotaxic centre lies in the pons.
It can moderate the respiratory rhythm centre.
Its signals shorten inspiration and so change the breathing rate.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q167",pn:167,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Respiratory volumes and capacities",q:`Which of the following statements are correct with respect to vital capacity?
      (a) It includes ERV, TV and IRV.
      (b) Total volume of air a person can inspire after a normal expiration.
      (c) The maximum volume of air a person can breathe in after forced expiration.
      (d) It includes ERV, RV and IRV.
      (e) The maximum volume of air a person can breathe out after a forced inspiration.
      Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["(a) and (e)","(b), (d) and (e)","(a), (c) and (d)","(a), (c) and (e)"],c:3,s:`Vital capacity is the maximum air a person can breathe in after a forced expiration.
It is also the maximum air breathed out after a forced inspiration.
It includes ERV, TV and IRV.
So (a), (c) and (e) are correct.`},{id:"neet-2022-q178",pn:178,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Respiratory system parts",q:"Which of the following is not the function of conducting part of respiratory system?",o:["Inhaled air is humidified","Temperature of inhaled air is brought to body temperature","Provides surface for diffusion of O2 and CO2","It clears inhaled air from foreign particles"],c:2,s:`The conducting part runs from the external nostrils to the terminal bronchioles.
It carries air to the alveoli, humidifies it, brings it to body temperature and removes foreign particles.
Gas diffusion happens in the exchange part (alveoli and their ducts), not in the conducting part.`},{id:"neet-2021-q155",pn:155,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Partial pressures of gases",q:"The partial pressures (in mm Hg) of oxygen (O\u2082) and carbon dioxide (CO\u2082) at alveoli (the site of diffusion) are:",o:["pO\u2082 = 159 and pCO\u2082 = 0.3","pO\u2082 = 104 and pCO\u2082 = 40","pO\u2082 = 40 and pCO\u2082 = 45","pO\u2082 = 95 and pCO\u2082 = 40"],c:1,s:`Alveolar air: pO\u2082 = 104 mm Hg, pCO\u2082 = 40 mm Hg.
159 and 0.3 are atmospheric values; 40 and 45 are deoxygenated blood; 95 and 40 are oxygenated blood.`},{id:"neet-2021-q185",pn:185,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Oxygen binding to haemoglobin",q:"Select the favourable conditions required for the formation of oxyhaemoglobin at the alveoli.",o:["Low pO\u2082, low pCO\u2082, more H\u207A, higher temperature","High pO\u2082, low pCO\u2082, less H\u207A, lower temperature","Low pO\u2082, high pCO\u2082, more H\u207A, higher temperature","High pO\u2082, high pCO\u2082, less H\u207A, higher temperature"],c:1,s:`At the alveoli pO\u2082 is high and pCO\u2082 is low.
Less H\u207A (higher pH) and lower temperature also favour binding of O\u2082 to haemoglobin.
That is high pO\u2082, low pCO\u2082, less H\u207A and lower temperature.
The reverse set of conditions occurs at tissues and releases O\u2082.`},{id:"neet-2021-q192",pn:192,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Altitude sickness",q:`Assertion (A): A person goes to high altitude and experiences 'altitude sickness' with symptoms like breathing difficulty and heart palpitations.
Reason (R): Due to low atmospheric pressure at high altitude, the body does not get sufficient oxygen. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["(A) is false but (R) is true","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false"],c:1,s:`At high altitude the atmospheric pressure, and so pO\u2082, is low, so the body gets less oxygen.
This causes breathing difficulty, fatigue and palpitations.
Both are true and R explains A.`},{id:"neet-2020-q001",pn:1,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Transport of oxygen",q:"Identify the wrong statement with reference to transport of oxygen.",o:["Partial pressure of CO2 can interfere with O2 binding with haemoglobin","Higher H+ conc. in alveoli favours the formation of oxyhaemoglobin","Low pCO2 in alveoli favours the formation of oxyhaemoglobin","Binding of oxygen with haemoglobin is mainly related to partial pressure of O2"],c:1,s:`Oxygen binds haemoglobin more when pO\u2082 is high, pCO\u2082 is low and H\u207A is low (alveoli).
So statement (2) is wrong: high H\u207A would lower the binding, not favour oxyhaemoglobin.`},{id:"neet-2020-q005",pn:5,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Mechanism of breathing",q:`Select the correct events that occur during inspiration.
(a) Contraction of diaphragm
(b) Contraction of external inter-coastal muscles
(c) Pulmonary volume decreases
(d) Intra pulmonary pressure increases`,o:["(c) and (d)","(a), (b) and (d)","only (d)","(a) and (b)"],c:3,s:`Inspiration: diaphragm and external intercostal muscles contract, the thoracic volume rises.
Pulmonary volume increases and intrapulmonary pressure falls below atmospheric, so air enters.
So (a) and (b) are correct; (c) and (d) are wrong.`},{id:"neet-2019-q004",pn:4,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Respiratory disorders",q:"Due to increasing air-borne allergens and pollutants, many people in urban areas are suffering from respiratory disorder causing wheezing due to :",o:["Proliferation of fibrous tissues and damage of the alveolar walls","Reduction in the secretion of surfactants by pneumocytes","Benign growth on mucous lining of nasal cavity","Inflammation of bronchi and bronchioles"],c:3,s:`Wheezing with difficulty in breathing is the sign of asthma.
Asthma is an allergic disorder.
It is caused by inflammation of bronchi and bronchioles.`},{id:"neet-2019-q027",pn:27,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Lung volumes and capacities",q:"Tidal Volume and Expiratory Reserve Volume of an athlete is 500 mL and 1000 mL respectively. What will be his Expiratory Capacity if the Residual Volume is 1200 mL ?",o:["2200 mL","2700 mL","1500 mL","1700 mL"],c:2,s:`Expiratory capacity = tidal volume + expiratory reserve volume.
= 500 + 1000 = 1500 mL.
Residual volume is not part of it.`},{id:"neet-2018-q169",pn:169,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Respiratory disorders",q:"Which of the following options correctly represents the lung conditions in asthma and emphysema, respectively?",o:["Inflammation of bronchioles; Decreased respiratory surface","Increased number of bronchioles; Increased respiratory surface","Increased respiratory surface; Inflammation of bronchioles","Decreased respiratory surface; Inflammation of bronchioles"],c:0,s:`Asthma is inflammation of bronchi and bronchioles that gives difficulty in breathing.
Emphysema damages the alveolar walls, so the respiratory surface decreases.
So asthma then emphysema is inflammation of bronchioles, then decreased respiratory surface.`},{id:"neet-2018-q171",pn:171,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Respiratory volumes",q:"Match the items given in Column I with those in Column II. Column I: a. Tidal volume, b. Inspiratory Reserve volume, c. Expiratory Reserve volume, d. Residual volume. Column II: i. 2500 \u2013 3000 mL, ii. 1100 \u2013 1200 mL, iii. 500 \u2013 550 mL, iv. 1000 \u2013 1100 mL",o:["a-iii, b-ii, c-i, d-iv","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-i, b-iv, c-ii, d-iii","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i"],c:1,s:`Tidal volume is about 500 mL: a\u2013iii (500\u2013550 mL).
Inspiratory reserve volume is 2500\u20133000 mL: b\u2013i.
Expiratory reserve volume is 1000\u20131100 mL: c\u2013iv.
Residual volume is 1100\u20131200 mL: d\u2013ii.`},{id:"neet-2018-q180",pn:180,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Occupational lung disease",q:"Which of the following is an occupational respiratory disorder?",o:["Anthracis","Silicosis","Botulism","Emphysema"],c:1,s:`Silicosis is caused by inhaling silica dust in stone grinding and quarrying, so it is occupational.
Anthrax and botulism are bacterial diseases, and emphysema is mainly caused by smoking.`}]})();
